(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-13-summarizing-the-evidence-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 13: Summarizing the Evidence Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A systematic review of observational studies of antioxidant vitamins to prevent cardiovascular disease combined the results of 12 studies to obtain a summary effect size and confidence interval. Which of the following would be the strongest rationale for combining study results?","choices":["You wish to obtain a more generalizable conclusion.","A statistical test shows that the studies are heterogeneous.","Most of the component studies are statistically significant.","The component studies have different, and to some extent complementary, biases.","You can obtain a more precise estimate of effect size."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The main advantage of combining (pooling) study results in a systematic review is to have a larger sample size and as a result a more stable and precise estimate of effect size. Although it could be argued that the results are somewhat more generalizable because they come from more than one time and place, generalizability is not the main advantage. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.1"},{"q":"You are asked to critique a review of the literature on whether alcohol is a risk factor for breast cancer. The reviewer has searched MEDLINE and found several observational studies of this question but has not searched elsewhere. All of the following are limitations of this search strategy except:","choices":["Studies with negative results tend to not be published.","MEDLINE searches typically miss some articles, even those included in the database.","MEDLINE does not include all of the world's journals.","MEDLINE can be simultaneously searched for both content area and methods."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. MEDLINE can be searched using both content and methods terms, but it does not include all of the world's journals, searches miss some of the articles in MEDLINE, and they are not a remedy for publication bias since all citations in it are published. These limitations are why reviewers need to use other, complementary ways of searching for articles in addition to MEDLINE. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.2"},{"q":"A systematic review of antiplatelet drugs for cardiovascular disease prevention combined individual patients, not trials. Which of the following is an advantage of this approach?","choices":["It allows for ecological comparisons.","It is more efficient for the investigator.","Subgroup analyses are possible.","It is not necessary to choose between fixed and random effects models when combining data.","Publication bias is less likely."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Although individual studies usually have limited statistical power in subgroups, pooling of several studies may overcome this problem, as long as patients, not trials, are pooled. Individual comparisons can avoid the ecological fallacy that can occur with comparing group characteristics. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is not generally used to define a specific clinical question studied by randomized controlled trials?","choices":["Covariates that were taken into account","Interventions (e.g., exposure or experimental treatment)","Comparison group (e.g., patients taking placebo in a randomized trial)","Outcomes","Patients in the trials"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. B–E are elements of PICO, core components of a specific research question. Covariates are a critical aspect of the research, especially for observational studies and to identify effect modification in clinical trials, but they are about how successfully the question can be answered, not the question itself. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.4"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes ways of measuring study quality?","choices":["The validity of summary measures of study quality is well established.","A description of study quality is a useful part of systematic reviews.","In a summary measure of quality, strengths of the study can make up for weaknesses.","Individual measures of quality such as randomization and blinding are not associated with study results."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systematic reviews should include a description of the methodologic strength of the studies to help users understand how strong the conclusions are. Combining individual elements of quality into a summary measure is less well established, perhaps because it is not meaningful to give each element the same weight. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.5"},{"q":"Which of the following kinds of studies is least likely to be published?","choices":["Small positive studies","Large negative studies","Large positive studies","Small negative studies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The usual situation is that small negative studies (ones that find no effect) are less likely to be published than small studies that find effects. Large studies are likely to be published no matter what they find. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.6"},{"q":"Which of the following is a comparative advantage of traditional (“narrative”) reviews over systematic reviews?","choices":["Readers can confirm that evidence cited is selected without bias.","Narrative reviews can review a broad range of questions bearing on the care of a condition.","They rely on the experience and judgment of an expert in the field.","They provide a quantitative summary of effects.","The scientific strength of the studies cited is explicitly evaluated."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Narrative reviews can take a comprehensive approach to the set of questions clinicians must answer to manage a patient but that is at the expense of a transparent description of the scientific basis for the evidence cited. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.7"},{"q":"Which of the following is not always part of a typical forest plot?","choices":["The number of studies that meet inclusion criteria for quality","A summary or pooled effect size with confidence interval","Point estimates of effect size for each study","Confidence intervals for each study","The size or weight contributed by each study"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Forest plots summarize the raw evidence in a systematic review. Pooled effect size and confidence interval may or may not be included, depending on whether it is appropriate to combine the study results. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.8"},{"q":"Which of the following cannot be used for identifying severity of illness as a reason for heterogeneity in a systematic review with meta-analysis?","choices":["Controlling for severity of illness in a study-level meta-analysis","A mathematical model relating average severity of illness to outcome across the component studies","A comparison of summary effect sizes in studies stratified by mean severity of illness","Subgroup analyses of patient-level data"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is not possible to control for a covariate in a study-level meta- analysis, as one might in one of the parent studies or in a patient-level meta- analysis. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.9"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best justification for combining the results of five studies into a single summary effect?","choices":["A statistical test does not detect heterogeneity.","All studies are of high quality.","The patients, interventions, and outcomes are relatively similar.","Publication bias has been ruled out by a funnel plot.","The random effects model will be used to calculate the summary effect size."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pooling is justified if there is relatively little heterogeneity across studies, as determined by an informed review of the patient, interventions, and outcomes. A statistical rest for heterogeneity is also useful in general, but not in this case, with so few studies, because of low statistical power. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.10"},{"q":"Which of the following is most correct for network meta-analyses?","choices":["Multiple interventions are directly compared to each other and ranked.","They require individual-level patient data for the analysis.","They require randomized controlled trials for analysis.","They do not require similar interventions for analysis.","They show the additional effects of new studies on the summary estimate."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Network meta-analyses can use observational or randomized data to obtain both direct and indirect comparisons among interventions or tests. They can use study-level or patient-level data for analysis. The interventions can be diverse, as long as they are on similar patients and address similar outcomes. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.11"},{"q":"Which of the following is an advantage of the random effects model over the fixed effect model?","choices":["It can be used for studies with time-to-event analyses.","It describes the summary effect size for a single, narrowly defined question.","It is better suited for meta-analyses of diagnostic test performance.","Confidence intervals tend to be narrower.","It gives more realistic results when there is heterogeneity among studies."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Random effect models take heterogeneity into account, at least if it is not extreme, and, therefore, result in wider confidence intervals, which are more likely to be accurate than the narrower confidence intervals that would result from a fixed effect model. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 13.12"}]};
